#%PAM-1.0
#
# PAM stack for margo's screen lockers — mlock and mshell's own lock
# screen — installed as /etc/pam.d/mlock.
#
# Both only call pam_authenticate to check the password of the user
# whose session is locked, so only the auth line matters. It is pam_unix
# alone on purpose: the system `login` stack mlock used before carries
# pam_faillock, and anyone at the locked screen could type wrong
# passwords until the owner's account was locked out everywhere. Like
# swaylock, a locker never needs account/session/password handling.
#
# Both answer the first hidden prompt with the typed password and fail
# on any further one (an OTP or second factor would otherwise be sent the
# password). Without this file they fall back to the `login` service.
#
# Fingerprint (optional): uncomment to accept a finger before the
# password. The locker blocks while fprintd waits for the sensor. mshell's
# lock screen talks to fprintd itself and does not need this line; with it,
# its password check waits for the sensor first.
#auth       sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
auth        required     pam_unix.so
